\f0\b\fs48 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Deep Research \'97 Thread 2 of 4: Volume Ruler (Hard Sets, MEV/MRV, Progression) for Helm\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf2 Executive Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 The Helm Volume Ruler provides a deterministic computational framework for tracking, allocating, and progressing training volume for muscle hypertrophy. Hypertrophy engines often suffer from two operational flaws: subjective volume modifications based on anecdotal user feedback and binary set accounting that either overcounts synergist movements\'97leading to under-prescribed direct work\'97or ignores them entirely, inducing systemic overtraining. The system specified herein resolves these flaws by implementing an offline-first calculation engine within 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PlanKit
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  that treats training volume as an explicit ledger of direct and fractional hard sets.\
By establishing strict mathematical boundaries for what constitutes a hard set, mapping compound exercise muscle contributions through fractional coefficients, and regulating weekly targets via an autoregulated Minimum Effective Volume (MEV) to Maximum Recoverable Volume (MRV) state machine, 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PlanKit
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  generates testable exercise prescriptions. The Large Language Model (LLM) conversational layer functions strictly as an interface to explain ledger state and propose bounded adaptations. All numerical modifications are calculated and clamped by the deterministic rules of the 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PlanKit
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  core engine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 Hard-Set Definition and Counting Rules\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 Primary Definition of a Hard Set\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 A hard set is defined within 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 PlanKit
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  as any completed exercise set executed within a load intensity of $30\\%\\text\{--\}85\\%$ of one-repetition maximum ($1\\text\{RM\}$), across a rep range of $5\\text\{--\}30$ repetitions, terminating at a proximity to failure of $0\\text\{--\}4$ Reps in Reserve ($\\text\{RIR\}$) (or Rating of Perceived Exertion ($\\text\{RPE\}$) $6\\text\{--\}10$). Sets executed below $30\\% \\text\{ 1RM\}$, at reps exceeding 30, or with an $\\text\{RIR\} > 4$ fail to generate sufficient mechanical tension or motor unit recruitment across high-threshold motor units to drive hypertrophic adaptations. Consequently, such sets are assigned a value of $0.00$ hard sets in the volume ledger.\

